    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking for some help with my Monstera deliciosa. It's been sitting in my living room for a year and doing great, but lately, the lower leaves are starting to turn a pale yellow color. The soil feels kind of damp even when I think I've watered it, but I don't see any obvious pests. Any idea what might be going on?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The pale yellowing is likely due to overwatering, as continuously damp soil prevents the roots from absorbing water properly. Even though you aren't seeing pests, root rot is a very common culprit for this specific decay pattern in Monstera plants. Try letting the soil dry out completely for a week before watering again to see if the new growth improves. If the lower leaves drop off rapidly or the stems feel mushy, you may need to trim the affected foliage or repot to fresh, well-draining soil.",
      "source": "target",
